      "page_idx": 11,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.085,
            0.724,
            0.184
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "If it is so important that hormones should be secreted in precise quantities, we need a mechanism through which this is done. The timing and amount of hormone released are regulated by feedback mechanisms. For example, if the sugar levels in blood rise, they are detected by the cells of the pancreas which respond by producing more insulin. As the blood sugar level falls, insulin secretion is reduced.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.141,
            0.194,
            0.299,
            0.217
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 6.4"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.226,
            0.898,
            0.261
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Hormones are secreted by endocrine glands and have specific functions. Complete Table 6.1 based on the hormone, the endocrine gland or the functions provided.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.12,
            0.295,
            0.901,
            0.512
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "table_caption",
              "index": 3,
              "bbox": [
                0.236,
                0.273,
                0.82,
                0.29
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Table 6.1 : Some important hormones and their functions"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "table_body",
              "index": 4,
              "bbox": [
                0.12,
                0.295,
                0.901,
                0.512
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_11_table_4.jpg",
              "content": "<table><tr><td>S.No</td><td>Hormone</td><td>Endocrine Gland</td><td>Functions</td></tr><tr><td>1.</td><td>Growth hormone</td><td>Pituitary gland</td><td>Stimulates growth in all organs</td></tr><tr><td>2.</td><td></td><td>Thyroid gland</td><td>Regulates metabolism for body growth</td></tr><tr><td>3.</td><td>Insulin</td><td></td><td>Regulates blood sugar level</td></tr><tr><td>4.</td><td>Testosterone</td><td>Testes</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>5.</td><td></td><td>Ovaries</td><td>Development of female sex organs,regulates menstrual cycle, etc.</td></tr><tr><td>6.</td><td>Adrenaline</td><td>Adrenal gland</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>7.</td><td rowspan=2>Releasinghormones</td><td rowspan=2></td><td rowspan=2>Stimulates pituitary gland to releasehormones</td></tr><tr><td></td></tr></table>"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.268,
            0.532,
            0.756,
            0.562
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Q U E S T I O N S"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.573,
            0.654,
            0.592
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. How does chemical coordination take place in animals?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.123,
            0.594,
            0.526,
            0.611
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. Why is the use of iodised salt advisable?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.614,
            0.774,
            0.632
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. How does our body respond when adrenaline is secreted into the blood?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.801,
            0.574,
            0.863,
            0.648
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 9,
              "bbox": [
                0.801,
                0.574,
                0.863,
                0.648
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_11_image_9.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.633,
            0.774,
            0.651
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. Why are some patients of diabetes treated by giving injections of insulin?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.159,
            0.663,
            0.541,
            0.69
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What you have learnt"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.137,
            0.698,
            0.891,
            0.731
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "n "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Control and coordination are the functions of the nervous system and hormones in our bodies.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.137,
            0.732,
            0.891,
            0.765
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "n "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The responses of the nervous system can be classified as reflex action, voluntary action or involuntary action.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.137,
            0.766,
            0.768,
            0.783
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "n "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The nervous system uses electrical impulses to transmit messages.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.784,
            0.891,
            0.816
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "n "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The nervous system gets information from our sense organs and acts through our muscles.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.137,
            0.818,
            0.694,
            0.835
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "n "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Chemical coordination is seen in both plants and animals.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.836,
            0.891,
            0.868
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Hormones produced in one part of an organism move to another part to achieve "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "the desired effect.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.87,
            0.716,
            0.888
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A feedback mechanism regulates the action of the hormones."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
